# Every version and checksum the hcw-lab image is built from, one per line.
#
# The Dockerfile copies this file in and sources it at the top of each RUN,
# so bumping a line here and pushing is the whole update procedure: CI
# rebuilds both targets, runs smoke.sh, and publishes. Nothing in the
# Dockerfiles repeats a version, with one exception noted at BASE_DIGEST.
#
# Every download is verified against the SHA256 beside it before it is used.
# The sums were resolved on 2026-09-25 from the publisher's own checksum file
# (releases.hashicorp.com SHA256SUMS, kubeconform CHECKSUMS, get.helm.sh
# .sha256sum, dl.k8s.io .sha256, PyPI's JSON API, the Terraform registry's
# download endpoint) and, for the GitHub source tarballs, computed locally
# from the downloaded archive. Every version was the newest stable release its
# publisher listed on that date (for the second azurerm mirror, the newest
# 4.x). Values are plain shell assignments; the file
# is read by /bin/sh inside the build and must stay that way (no export, no
# expansions, no spaces around '=').
#
# 2026-10-14 (#1133, the weekly image scan): terraform, azurerm 5.x, azapi
# and the Azure CLI moved to their publishers' newest releases, the sums read
# the same ways that day. The other mirrored providers and tools the scan
# named (azurerm 4.x, alz, random, modtm, time, kubeconform, helm, kubectl)
# were already at their newest release; their findings are Go library
# versions only a publisher's rebuild can move, and the weekly scan says when.
#
# 2026-10-31 (#1426 again): the base moved to python:3.14.10-slim-trixie,
# terraform to 1.17.1, kubectl to 1.37.2 and azurerm 5.x to 5.12.0, the sums
# read the same ways that day; AZURE_CLI_PYTHON_OVERRIDES is new and says why.

# Base image, on every FROM in lab-image/Dockerfile: the Docker Official
# Image python:3.14.10-slim-trixie, CPython 3.14.10 built on
# debian:trixie-slim (Debian 13, point release 13.7: /etc/debian_version in
# the image built from this digest on 2026-10-31). The full tag is written
# because the tag beside a digest is documentation and this one names the
# patch release.
#
# Resolved 2026-10-31 (#1426). The patch tag and the line tag
# python:3.14-slim-trixie had different index digests (an index is the list
# of per-platform images behind one tag): the line tag also lists a riscv64
# image, still the 3.14.9 build, which the patch tag does not list yet. The
# linux/amd64 image, the only one this build uses, is the same manifest in
# both (sha256:ec6a14df5b63e3fbb3498e3240f7f640c230f9dca67683d23265db3866697f2d),
# and its config sets PYTHON_VERSION=3.14.10. So the pin is the patch tag's
# own index digest. That digest will change when a riscv64 3.14.10 build is
# added to the patch tag (it has moved once already: #1426 first recorded
# sha256:fa99b651...). That is harmless, because the pin is a digest and does
# not follow the tag: once the two tags list the same images, the weekly
# image-base bump opens one ordinary pull request to the new digest, with the
# same linux/amd64 image inside, and then stops, so the bump does not loop.
#
# One base for every stage, the build-only fetch, vendor and mirror stages
# included, rather than debian:trixie-slim for the stages that need no
# Python: the python image IS debian:trixie-slim plus CPython (its linux/amd64
# manifest names debian:trixie-slim's own amd64 digest as its base), so the
# saving would be about 40 MB in stages that never ship, against a second
# digest to keep in step; and vendor-avm.sh then runs at build time on the
# same Python 3.14 the runner ships instead of Debian's python3 (3.13 in
# trixie). No Debian python3 package is installed anywhere in the image.
#
# The FROM lines carry this same reference, because a FROM cannot read a
# file; the publish workflow fails the build if any FROM that is not an
# earlier stage differs from BASE_IMAGE@BASE_DIGEST. The digest is the image
# INDEX digest, from the registry's Docker-Content-Digest header (the method
# vps-agent/lib/capabilities.js documents), cross-checked with
# `docker buildx imagetools inspect`, which reported the same value.
# BASE_PYTHON_VERSION is the CPython release that digest carries; smoke.sh
# compares `python3 --version` with it, so a digest bump that moves Python
# fails until this line moves too. The owner's floor is the newest 3.x line
# at no more than two patch releases behind its latest
# (scripts/version-floors.json holds the numbers).
BASE_IMAGE=python:3.14.10-slim-trixie
BASE_DIGEST=sha256:3cabfd7d7aaf394e6040e79fa1eec6ca1c52b74ed9188650472695d9a9326999
BASE_PYTHON_VERSION=3.14.10

# Base of lab-image/sandbox-template/Dockerfile, Docker's Claude Code sandbox
# template (Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS "resolute" on 2026-09-25). Same rule and same
# check as BASE_IMAGE; resolved the same two ways, and unchanged since the
# recipe landed with #676.
SANDBOX_BASE_IMAGE=docker/sandbox-templates:claude-code
SANDBOX_BASE_DIGEST=sha256:30ec2b1bb76e3993aff912c553a5c249ad95b595249a32fde7158eafb71bda25

# Tools in the runner target.
TERRAFORM_VERSION=1.17.1
TERRAFORM_SHA256=4638628058e71a55212b1c563712eac03b902128f40740f3dc8174df12135061
KUBECONFORM_VERSION=0.8.0
KUBECONFORM_SHA256=9bc2bffbf71f261128533edaf912153948b7ff238f9a531ae6d34466ec287883
HELM_VERSION=4.3.1
HELM_SHA256=0403a88ae20913d11b10a2467b50087721fea7581736da6b9d3992f5357fe54b
# ansible-core, the newest stable line. 2.21's controller range is Python
# 3.12 to 3.14 in the support matrix
# (https://docs.ansible.com/projects/ansible/latest/reference_appendices/release_and_maintenance.html,
# read 2026-09-25), and its wheel declares Requires-Python >=3.12; 2.22 was
# at 2.22.0b1, a beta. These three ANSIBLE_CORE_* lines are also the lab
# host's: lab-host/bootstrap.sh keeps no copy and installs ansible-core from
# them with --require-hashes (LAB-13, #959). The weekly
# `chore/lab-pins-image-base` pull request moves them within their release
# line; a new line is moved by hand.
ANSIBLE_CORE_VERSION=2.21.5
ANSIBLE_CORE_SHA256=08886f9f3e23d129aa98a01271c4a47833516e1cb7c737a246788cb474f41222
# ansible-core's dependencies and theirs, each the newest release inside the
# range its dependant declares, as the one wheel pip selects for CPython 3.14
# on linux x86_64 (cryptography publishes six manylinux x86_64 wheels; this is
# the cp311-abi3-manylinux_2_34 one). One per line in pip's requirements
# syntax. The fetch stage downloads them, and the runner installs them, in
# pip's hash-checking mode: --require-hashes refuses a wheel whose SHA256
# differs, and also refuses to resolve any dependency that is not listed here
# with a hash, so a new transitive dependency fails the build instead of
# arriving unpinned.
ANSIBLE_CORE_DEPS='
jinja2==3.1.6 --hash=sha256:85ece4451f492d0c13c5dd7c13a64681a86afae63a5f347908daf103ce6d2f67
markupsafe==3.0.3 --hash=sha256:457a69a9577064c05a97c41f4e65148652db078a3a509039e64d3467b9e7ef97
pyyaml==6.0.3 --hash=sha256:c458b6d084f9b935061bc36216e8a69a7e293a2f1e68bf956dcd9e6cbcd143f5
cryptography==50.0.1 --hash=sha256:51afcfceb15597cf2635068e4ac9a56b2abde622edde17f37d85fd7b5306497a
cffi==2.1.1 --hash=sha256:b0431303acaea1089ad4b3e9ce4e6518193def1118d4073ca848635ee4ea2e96
pycparser==3.0 --hash=sha256:b727414169a36b7d524c1c3e31839a521725078d7b2ff038656844266160a992
packaging==26.3 --hash=sha256:d7193f7c8e4e93f444fde0262bf90af30e16fa0ad0ad44cb553c87339b23cd1c
resolvelib==1.2.1 --hash=sha256:fb06b66c8da04172d9e72a21d7d06186d8919e32ae5ab5cdf5b9d920be805ac2
'

# Kubernetes JSON schemas for kubeconform, so the `kubeconform` capability
# validates with no network (#675): one directory of
# github.com/yannh/kubernetes-json-schema (the repository kubeconform reads
# over HTTPS by default) at a pinned commit, mirrored to
# /opt/kubeconform/schemas/<dir> under the name kubeconform's own location
# template expects. The commit is content-addressed; the SHA256 is the tree
# hash of the checked-out directory by the same pipeline AVM_CHILD_MODULES
# documents below, so a moved branch and a changed file both fail the build.
# The directory names the same Kubernetes line as KUBECTL_VERSION (1.37 for
# 1.37.2). A kubectl patch release does not move it. A new line moves both, by
# hand, in one pull request: the weekly bump cannot, because no publisher
# serves a checksum for this commit, so it says so in the weekly issue, and a
# test fails when the two sit on different lines (#1323).
KUBERNETES_JSON_SCHEMA_COMMIT=a6f9a32d2ccb64b6e4f5b41419b9c2e8ee0cce18
KUBERNETES_JSON_SCHEMA_DIR=v1.37.1-standalone-strict
KUBERNETES_JSON_SCHEMA_SHA256=fc44a2541c357219c7253e12c2667d20edf088e920123806fe48e5aa6d156fc9

# Tools added by the full target.
KUBECTL_VERSION=1.37.2
KUBECTL_SHA256=c04487523e7aa7d1493f7808174c899db93d7a9cf57531420cd76b9bd415f8f4
# Azure CLI: the upstream release, installed from Microsoft's apt repository
# as the package `azure-cli=<version>-1~<suite>` that Microsoft's install page
# documents. packages.microsoft.com publishes no trixie suite: on 2026-09-25
# https://packages.microsoft.com/repos/azure-cli/dists/ listed bookworm,
# bullseye, jammy, noble and resolute among others and no trixie, and
# https://learn.microsoft.com/cli/azure/install-azure-cli-linux?pivots=apt
# lists Debian 11 and 12 as tested and says, under "No package for your
# distribution", to use the latest Debian suite, bookworm. So the full target
# installs the bookworm build on trixie (AZURE_CLI_SUITE). That build's
# Depends (libc6 >= 2.35, libssl3 >= 3.0.0, libffi8, zlib1g, libbz2-1.0,
# libuuid1, libgcc-s1) are met by trixie's own packages, and the CLI runs on
# the Python it bundles under /opt/az, not on the image's. The sandbox
# template installs the same release from its base's own suite (resolute).
# The apt signing key is verified by SHA256 before it is trusted; it is the
# key the bookworm and resolute suites are signed with.
AZURE_CLI_VERSION=2.91.0
AZURE_CLI_SUITE=bookworm
MICROSOFT_APT_KEY_SHA256=2fa9c05d591a1582a9aba276272478c262e95ad00acf60eaee1644d93941e3c6

# Python packages replaced inside the Azure CLI's own Python (/opt/az), one
# per line in pip's requirements syntax, each the newest release with the
# SHA256 of its one wheel (py3-none-any) from PyPI's JSON API, checked
# against the downloaded file on 2026-10-31.
#
# Why: the weekly image scan (#1426) found every CRITICAL and HIGH Python
# finding of the full image inside the Azure CLI package, not in anything
# this file pins. Azure CLI 2.91.0, the newest release in Microsoft's apt
# repository that day, bundles certifi 2024.7.4, PyJWT 2.13.0, urllib3 2.7.0
# and cryptography 48.0.1 (read from the .deb's own dist-info folders), and
# its pip 26.2.1 carries its own private copies of certifi 2026.6.17,
# msgpack 1.1.2, setuptools 70.3.0 and urllib3 2.7.0 (pip/_vendor/vendor.txt).
# The CLI's own packages accept the newer versions: msal asks for
# PyJWT[crypto] <3,>=1.0.0, requests for urllib3 <3,>=1.26, and nothing caps
# certifi. Microsoft's dev branch already moves certifi and PyJWT
# (requirements.py3.Linux.txt: certifi 2026.10.19, PyJWT 2.15.0).
#
# What stays until Microsoft releases a new CLI, and is not replaced here:
#   cryptography 48.0.1: the bundled pyOpenSSL 26.2.0 declares
#     cryptography <49,>=46.0.0, so a newer cryptography alone would break a
#     package the CLI imports. Newer pyOpenSSL releases lift that cap (26.3.0
#     asks for >=49,<50 and 26.4.0 for >=49,<51), but Microsoft still pins
#     pyOpenSSL 26.2.0 and has not tested the CLI on 26.3 or later, which
#     removed X509Req, dump_certificate_request and load_certificate_request
#     and deprecated the X509 setters the CLI's `az ad sp create-for-rbac
#     --create-cert` path uses. So neither is replaced here. Neither open
#     finding on 48.0.1 is reachable from the CLI: CVE-2026-69247 (fixed in
#     50.0.0) is in pkcs7_decrypt_der/pem/smime, which the CLI never calls,
#     and CVE-2026-69249 (fixed in 49.0.0) is slow certificate path building
#     in cryptography.x509.verification, a denial of service, which the CLI
#     does not use either (the advisories read on https://osv.dev, the
#     pyOpenSSL changelog and the CLI 2.91.0 .deb's own code on 2026-10-31).
#     Microsoft's dev branch still pins 48.0.1.
#   oauthlib 3.2.2 (a trivy scan of this build on 2026-10-31 reports
#     CVE-2026-118669, fixed in 4.0.0): a new major version, under
#     requests-oauthlib 1.2.0, and Microsoft's dev branch still pins 3.2.2.
#     The advisory is in the server-side token grant handlers, which a
#     client like the CLI does not run.
#   pip's private copies of msgpack 1.1.2, setuptools 70.3.0 and urllib3
#     2.7.0: pip 26.2.2 (below, the newest pip) still carries the same three;
#     only certifi moved in it. They wait on a pip release. pip itself stays,
#     because `az extension add` installs extensions with it.
#
# The fetch stage downloads these wheels and the full target installs them
# into /opt/az with the CLI's own Python in pip's hash-checking mode, in the
# same step that installs the CLI, then runs `pip check` there. Before it
# installs, the build stops if the CLI already ships a package at or above
# the version listed here (or no longer ships it at all), naming the line to
# remove: the weekly bump moves AZURE_CLI_VERSION alone, and without that
# check a newer CLI would be quietly downgraded to the older version listed.
# Remove a line once AZURE_CLI_VERSION ships that version or newer. An empty
# block, or no block at all, is fine: the fetch, the install and smoke.sh
# then skip the replacement and change nothing else.
# scripts/lab-image-azure-cli-overrides.test.mjs checks each line's shape.
AZURE_CLI_PYTHON_OVERRIDES='
certifi==2026.10.23 --hash=sha256:e9b54de76e530bb6cf0ccd036787103acb58fd42ba930ea8bf948ddbaa2f61d1
pyjwt==2.15.1 --hash=sha256:42d59d631f7768a1028a64c7ff581a9bf7519804daf91fc5b6c56e30eec5e193
urllib3==2.8.0 --hash=sha256:0cf3cae568d36aa9576b28dfb35f11328f1cb974ca7647d9475ebb86c75ac6e3
pip==26.2.2 --hash=sha256:fd7f4dfa8190e014013b9f08f3e973f4e4c26ffd179a204895533a7254813a70
'

# Terraform providers in the filesystem mirror at /opt/terraform/mirror, each
# with the SHA256 of its linux_amd64 zip as the registry reports it.
# azurerm is mirrored twice: 5.x is current, and the AVM modules vendored
# below still constrain to ~> 4.0 / ~> 4.35. modtm and time are not
